---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Clock auction simulator';
const description =
  'An interactive ascending clock auction: watch price discovery round by round, see how the activity rule punishes demand reduction, and compare revenue against the efficient allocation.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 pt-28 pb-16 sm:px-8 sm:pt-32">
    <a href="/projects/clock-auction" class="text-[13px] text-ink-3 hover:text-brand">
      &lt;&lt; cd ../about-this
    </a>

    <h1 class="glow mt-4 text-3xl font-800 tracking-tight text-brand sm:text-4xl">
      ./clock-auction
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-[14px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
      An ascending clock auction for airport landing slots. Prices rise on any product with more
      demand than supply; the auction ends when nothing is oversubscribed. Bidders are simulated,
      so you can rerun it and change the rules.
    </p>

    <div id="ca" class="mt-8"></div>

    <details class="term term-corners mt-10">
      <summary class="term-bar cursor-pointer select-none">what to look for</summary>
      <div class="space-y-3 p-5 text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
        <p>
          <span class="text-brand">The activity rule is the whole game.</span> Eligibility is
          measured in points and you must keep using at least the configured share of it. What
          you do not use, you lose permanently. Set the rule to 100% and bidders are locked into
          their opening position. Drop it toward 0% and they can sit back and re-enter late,
          which destroys price discovery.
        </p>
        <p>
          <span class="text-brand">Demand reduction.</span> Meridian deliberately bids for less
          than it wants once a product looks contested, to slow the clock. It often pays less per
          slot. Watch whether it also ends up with fewer slots than it should have, and what that
          does to the efficiency figure.
        </p>
        <p>
          <span class="text-brand">Increment size trades speed against overshoot.</span> Large
          steps clear in few rounds but blow past the price where demand actually balanced. That
          gap is revenue the winners did not need to pay, and it is why real auctions shrink the
          increment as they converge.
        </p>
        <p>
          <span class="text-brand">Efficiency is not revenue.</span> The two move independently. A
          design can raise more money while putting slots in the wrong hands, and the scoreboard
          shows both so the tradeoff is visible rather than assumed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import { mountClockAuction } from '@/scripts/clock-auction-ui';
  const el = document.getElementById('ca');
  if (el) mountClockAuction(el);
</script>
